TI  - Aletheia: Permission-Minimality Testing for Coding-Agent Rules
AU  - Jieke Shi
AU  - Yuchen Chen
AU  - Junda He
AU  - Yue Liu
AU  - David Lo
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-09-30
AB  - Coding agents powered by Large Language Models (LLMs) read repository rules (e.g., AGENTS.md and Cursor rules) to learn project conventions and development procedures. An attacker can exploit the same files to mislead the agent into requesting excessive permissions or performing unrelated operations, such as accessing credentials or transferring data. We present Aletheia, a framework for permission-minimality testing of coding-agent rules. It tests whether the permissions requested in these rules are necessary to complete specified tasks. An LLM first translates the rule files into a typed permission language. Aletheia then removes one permission declaration at a time and synthesizes the corresponding restricted sandbox configuration using a trusted task environment. It executes the unchanged rule and task under both the full and restricted configurations. If both executions produce artifacts that pass the functional tests, Aletheia identifies the removed permission as unnecessary for the tested task and flags the corresponding rule file as suspicious. We evaluate Aletheia on AIShellJack, which provides 314 attack rules containing prompt-injection payloads and five benign rules, as well as 80 manually verified benign rules from GHAgentFiles. Aletheia detects all 314 attacks while reporting only three false positives among the 85 benign rules (3.5%). On average, the evaluation takes 44.35 seconds and costs US$0.22 per rule.
